      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "add per-conversation whatsapp debounce config",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe existing WhatsApp debounce setting is too broad for mixed usage.\n\nSome conversations should be processed immediately, while others should wait briefly so OpenClaw can batch several short messages into one agent turn. This matters for owner chats, external direct chats, and busy groups that need different timing.\n\nAI-assisted: yes, Codex helped update this branch and evidence.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds per-conversation inbound debounce settings for WhatsApp direct chats and groups while keeping the existing channel-level debounce as the fallback.\n\nThe runtime now supports:\n\n- `channels.whatsapp.direct[\"<id>\"].debounceMs`\n- `channels.whatsapp.direct[\"*\"].debounceMs`\n- `channels.whatsapp.groups[\"<id>\"].debounceMs`\n- `channels.whatsapp.groups[\"*\"].debounceMs`\n- account-scoped direct/group maps that replace the matching root map for that account\n\nA review found the first implementation read the listener startup config snapshot. This branch now resolves conversation debounce through the live config loader so config changes apply without reconnecting the WhatsApp listener.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators can tune WhatsApp batching per conversation without changing the global WhatsApp debounce for every chat.\n\nExamples:\n\n- owner/admin direct chats can use `debounceMs: 0` for immediate turns\n- external direct chats can wait longer for short message bursts\n- busy groups can have their own window\n- wildcard entries can set direct/group defaults\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent head: `82c528450fb4f4fa02ed082dd7645c7c96494912`\nCurrent base checked locally: `b0ebb81e89f5217397d409c996e2bbf914623a94` (`origin/main`)\n\nThis update merged latest `origin/main` into the PR branch. The latest merge was clean.\n\nLatest validation:\n\n- `TMPDIR=\"$PWD/.tmp\" node --import tsx scripts/generate-bundled-channel-config-metadata.ts --check` - passed\n- `TMPDIR=\"$PWD/.tmp\" node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.config.ts src/config/zod-schema.providers-whatsapp.test.ts` - 1 file, 11 tests passed\n- `TMPDIR=\"$PWD/.tmp\" node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.e2e.config.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/auto-reply.web-auto-reply.connection-and-logging.e2e.test.ts -t \"resolves conversation-scoped debounce overrides\"` - 1 test passed, 28 skipped\n- `git diff --cached --check` - passed before commit\n- `git diff --check` - passed before commit\n\nReview notes:\n\n- `codex review --base origin/main` found one actionable issue: the debounce resolver used a stale config snapshot. This update fixed it and expanded the e2e test to prove a later config change changes the resolved debounce.\n- A follow-up `codex review --base origin/main` on the fixed, merged diff did not finish on this machine and was stopped after several minutes; the bundled autoreview helper also refused the local staged diff because it was over the review size limit.\n- A direct local run of the full `extensions/whatsapp/src/auto-reply.web-auto-reply.connection-and-logging.e2e.test.ts` file earlier still failed older connection/logging assertions, while the new debounce override test passed by name.\n- Local Testbox/Crabbox was unavailable in this sweep, so these were local wrapper checks.\n",
        "branch": "whatsapp-conversation-debounce",
